Important: This is a UI and navigation implementation, not an authorization system. Hiding a menu item does not prevent a user from accessing its URL. Protected controllers and actions must still enforce authorization on the server.

### 1\. Understanding the sidebar

A sidebar is a vertical navigation area positioned along the side of an application. It provides access to major application features while leaving the central area available for page content.

Common examples include:

* HR systems — employee records, attendance, leave applications.

* Inventory systems — items, warehouses, stock movements.

* Approval systems — pending requests, approvals, audit history.

* Employee management systems — employee profiles, departments, reports.

* Dashboard applications — analytics, reports, settings, administration.

### Sidebar versus top navigation

| Feature               | Sidebar               | Top navigation                  |
| --------------------- | --------------------- | ------------------------------- |
| Many menu items       | Works well            | Can become crowded              |
| Persistent navigation | Yes, on desktop       | Yes                             |
| Content width         | Uses horizontal space | More space for content          |
| Nested menus          | Natural fit           | Often less convenient           |
| Mobile behavior       | Usually slides in     | Often collapses into a dropdown |

For enterprise applications, a sidebar is useful when users repeatedly switch between several functional areas. A top bar still has a role: it can contain the hamburger button, page title, theme control, and user actions.

### 2\. Project structure and responsibilities

In an ASP.NET Core MVC application, the sidebar should normally live in the shared layout rather than be copied into every page.

| File or folder              | Responsibility                                                        |
| --------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Views/Shared/_Layout.cshtml | Shared sidebar, top bar, content container, and script references     |
| wwwroot/css/site.css        | Theme colors, sidebar layout, responsive rules, and transitions       |
| wwwroot/js/site.js          | Sidebar behavior, theme switching, and preference persistence         |
| Controllers/                | MVC actions that handle navigation requests                           |
| Views/Home/                 | Dashboard and home page Razor views                                   |
| Views/Employee/             | Employee-related views                                                |
| wwwroot/lib/                | Local client-side libraries, including Bootstrap if installed locally |
| Program.cs                  | MVC registration, routing, and middleware configuration               |

