### Before you begin

* Open your existing ASP.NET Core MVC project in Visual Studio.

* Confirm that it targets `net8.0`.

* Locate `Views/Shared/_Layout.cshtml`, `wwwroot/css/site.css`, and `wwwroot/js/site.js`.

* Back up your current layout and JavaScript, especially the working theme toggle.

* Check whether Bootstrap 5 and Bootstrap Icons or Font Awesome are already installed.

Do not create duplicate files if these already exist. The following implementation replaces or merges the relevant sections of those files.

### 3\. Create the sidebar layout

Open `Views/Shared/_Layout.cshtml`.

A shared layout is the right place for navigation because every view using the layout will automatically display the same sidebar. ASP.NET Core MVC renders the individual page where `@RenderBody()` appears.

### Step 3.1 — Understand the layout

We'll use these main sections:

* `<aside>` — contains the sidebar.

* `<nav>` — contains navigation links.

* `<header>` — contains the hamburger button, page heading, and theme toggle.

* `<main>` — contains the current MVC view.

* An overlay — lets mobile users dismiss the sidebar by tapping outside it.

### Step 3.2 — Replace `_Layout.cshtml`

The following is the complete layout for this tutorial. It assumes the standard MVC Tag Helpers are enabled in `Views/_ViewImports.cshtml`.

```cshtml

@{
    var currentController =
        ViewContext.RouteData.Values["controller"]?.ToString() ?? "Home";

    var currentAction =
        ViewContext.RouteData.Values["action"]?.ToString() ?? "Index";

    var pageTitle = ViewData["Title"]?.ToString() ?? "Dashboard";

    var activeSection = currentController.ToLowerInvariant();
}

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" data-theme="light">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />

    <title>@pageTitle - Enterprise Portal</title>

    @* Load the saved theme before the page is painted. *@
    <script>
        (function () {
            try {
                const savedTheme = localStorage.getItem("themePreference");
                const theme = savedTheme === "dark" ? "dark" : "light";
                document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", theme);
            } catch {
                document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", "light");
            }
        })();
    </script>

    @* Bootstrap 5 - use your existing local version if available. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet"
          href="~/lib/bootstrap/dist/css/bootstrap.min.css" />

    @* Bootstrap Icons. For production, consider hosting a pinned version locally. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet"
          href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap-icons@1.11.3/font/bootstrap-icons.min.css" />

    <link rel="stylesheet" href="~/css/site.css" asp-append-version="true" />
</head>

<body>
    <div class="app-shell">

        @* Mobile overlay. *@
        <button type="button"
                id="sidebarOverlay"
                class="sidebar-overlay"
                aria-label="Close navigation"
                tabindex="-1"
                hidden>
        </button>

        @* Sidebar. *@
        <aside id="appSidebar"
               class="app-sidebar"
               aria-label="Main navigation">

            <div class="sidebar-brand">
                <a asp-controller="Home"
                   asp-action="Index"
                   class="brand-link"
                   aria-label="Enterprise Portal home">
                    <span class="brand-logo">
                        <i class="bi bi-grid-1x2-fill" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    </span>

                    <span class="sidebar-label brand-name">
                        Enterprise Portal
                    </span>
                </a>

                <button type="button"
                        id="sidebarClose"
                        class="sidebar-close mobile-only"
                        aria-label="Close navigation">
                    <i class="bi bi-x-lg" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                </button>
            </div>

            <div class="sidebar-caption sidebar-label">
                WORKSPACE
            </div>

            <nav class="sidebar-nav">
                <a asp-controller="Home"
                   asp-action="Index"
                   class="sidebar-link @(activeSection == "home" ? "active" : "")"
                   aria-current="@(activeSection == "home" ? "page" : null)"
                   title="Dashboard">
                    <i class="bi bi-speedometer2" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    <span class="sidebar-label">Dashboard</span>
                </a>

                <a asp-controller="Profile"
                   asp-action="Index"
                   class="sidebar-link @(activeSection == "profile" ? "active" : "")"
                   aria-current="@(activeSection == "profile" ? "page" : null)"
                   title="Profile">
                    <i class="bi bi-person-circle" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    <span class="sidebar-label">Profile</span>
                </a>

                <a asp-controller="Employee"
                   asp-action="Index"
                   class="sidebar-link @(activeSection == "employee" ? "active" : "")"
                   aria-current="@(activeSection == "employee" ? "page" : null)"
                   title="Employees">
                    <i class="bi bi-people" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    <span class="sidebar-label">Employees</span>
                </a>

                <a asp-controller="Reports"
                   asp-action="Index"
                   class="sidebar-link @(activeSection == "reports" ? "active" : "")"
                   aria-current="@(activeSection == "reports" ? "page" : null)"
                   title="Reports">
                    <i class="bi bi-bar-chart-line" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    <span class="sidebar-label">Reports</span>
                </a>
            </nav>

            <div class="sidebar-caption sidebar-label">
                PREFERENCES
            </div>

            <nav class="sidebar-nav" aria-label="Preferences">
                <a asp-controller="Settings"
                   asp-action="Index"
                   class="sidebar-link @(activeSection == "settings" ? "active" : "")"
                   aria-current="@(activeSection == "settings" ? "page" : null)"
                   title="Settings">
                    <i class="bi bi-gear" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    <span class="sidebar-label">Settings</span>
                </a>
            </nav>

            <div class="sidebar-footer">
                @* Logout should submit a POST request in a real app. *@
                <form asp-controller="Account"
                      asp-action="Logout"
                      method="post"
                      class="logout-form">
                    <button type="submit"
                            class="sidebar-link sidebar-logout"
                            title="Logout">
                        <i class="bi bi-box-arrow-right"
                           aria-hidden="true"></i>
                        <span class="sidebar-label">Logout</span>
                    </button>
                </form>

                <button type="button"
                        id="sidebarCollapse"
                        class="sidebar-collapse desktop-only"
                        aria-label="Collapse sidebar"
                        aria-expanded="true"
                        title="Collapse sidebar">
                    <i class="bi bi-layout-sidebar-inset"
                       aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    <span class="sidebar-label">Collapse sidebar</span>
                </button>
            </div>
        </aside>

        @* Main content area. *@
        <div id="appMain" class="app-main">

            <header class="topbar">
                <div class="topbar-left">
                    <button type="button"
                            id="sidebarToggle"
                            class="icon-button mobile-only"
                            aria-label="Open navigation"
                            aria-controls="appSidebar"
                            aria-expanded="false">
                        <i class="bi bi-list" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    </button>

                    <div>
                        <div class="page-heading">@pageTitle</div>
                        <div class="page-subheading">
                            Enterprise Management System
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="topbar-actions">
                    <button type="button"
                            id="themeToggle"
                            class="icon-button theme-toggle"
                            aria-label="Switch to dark theme"
                            title="Switch theme">
                        <i class="bi bi-moon-stars-fill"
                           aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    </button>
                </div>
            </header>

            <main id="mainContent" class="page-content" tabindex="-1">
                @RenderBody()
            </main>

            <footer class="app-footer">
                <span>Enterprise Portal</span>
                <span>&copy; @DateTime.Now.Year</span>
            </footer>
        </div>
    </div>

    @* Load Bootstrap before any page-specific scripts that depend on it. *@
    <script src="~/lib/bootstrap/dist/js/bootstrap.bundle.min.js"></script>
    <script src="~/js/site.js" asp-append-version="true"></script>

    @await RenderSectionAsync("Scripts", required: false)
</body>
</html>
```

### Why these elements matter

| Element or attribute      | Purpose                                                                     |
| ------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| asp-controller            | Identifies the MVC controller.                                              |
| asp-action                | Identifies the controller action.                                           |
| aria-label                | Provides an accessible name for controls and navigation.                    |
| aria-current="page"       | Identifies the current navigation link to assistive technology.             |
| @RenderBody()             | Inserts the current view into the layout.                                   |
| @RenderSectionAsync()     | Allows individual views to add page-specific scripts.                       |
| asp-append-version="true" | Adds a file version query string to help browsers fetch updated CSS and JS. |
| data-theme                | Controls which CSS theme variables are active.                              |

The `activeSection` value uses the current controller name, so the Employees menu stays highlighted for actions such as `Employee/Details` as well as `Employee/Index`.

Note: The Logout link is a POST form because signing out changes authentication state. Connect it to your real sign-out implementation. Do not add a fake logout action that only redirects without clearing the authentication session.

### Step 3.3 — Check Tag Helpers

Open `Views/_ViewImports.cshtml`. Ensure it contains:

```cshtml

@using YourProjectName
@using YourProjectName.Models
@addTagHelper *, Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.TagHelpers
```

Replace `YourProjectName` with your project's actual namespace. If the existing file already imports the correct namespaces and Tag Helpers, keep it as is.

### 4\. Configure the MVC navigation destinations

The sidebar generates links, but each destination must correspond to a real controller and action.

For example:

`asp-controller="Employee"` and `asp-action="Index"` normally generate a link to the `Index` action of `EmployeeController`.

If your application already has equivalent controllers, use those rather than creating duplicates.

### Example controller

Place this in `Controllers/EmployeeController.cs`:

```csharp

using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

namespace YourProjectName.Controllers
{
    public class EmployeeController : Controller
    {
        public IActionResult Index()
        {
            ViewData["Title"] = "Employees";
            return View();
        }
    }
}
```

Place a corresponding view at `Views/Employee/Index.cshtml`:

```cshtml

@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Employees";
}

<div class="content-card">
    <h2>Employee Management</h2>
    <p>Welcome to the employee management module.</p>
</div>
```

Repeat the pattern for Profile, Reports, and Settings if these modules do not already exist. For the Dashboard, the standard template commonly provides `HomeController.Index()`.

### Example menu routing

| Menu      | Controller         | Action        |
| --------- | ------------------ | ------------- |
| Dashboard | HomeController     | Index         |
| Profile   | ProfileController  | Index         |
| Employees | EmployeeController | Index         |
| Reports   | ReportsController  | Index         |
| Settings  | SettingsController | Index         |
| Logout    | AccountController  | Logout (POST) |

MVC routing, rather than manually concatenated URLs, lets ASP.NET Core generate the appropriate link. It also makes the application easier to maintain if routes change.

