### What the main CSS properties do

| Property                      | Why it is used                                                    |
| ----------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |
| position: fixed               | Keeps the sidebar in place while the page scrolls.                |
| inset: 0 auto 0 0             | Anchors the sidebar to the top, bottom, and left of the viewport. |
| z-index                       | Controls which layer appears in front of another.                 |
| overflow-y: auto              | Allows long navigation menus to scroll independently.             |
| width                         | Defines expanded and collapsed sidebar sizes.                     |
| margin-left                   | Moves the desktop content beside the sidebar.                     |
| transform: translateX()       | Slides the mobile sidebar offscreen or onscreen.                  |
| visibility and pointer-events | Prevent interaction with a hidden mobile sidebar.                 |
| transition                    | Animates changes in width, color, and position.                   |
| @media                        | Applies different layout rules at different screen widths.        |
| CSS variables                 | Keep theme colors consistent and easy to change.                  |

### Understanding the breakpoints

Desktop — 992px and wider

Sidebar stays visible. Users can collapse it to icons only.

Tablet — 768px to 991px

Sidebar defaults to compact mode and can be expanded using the collapse control.

Mobile — below 768px

Sidebar is hidden until opened with the hamburger button. An overlay closes it.

These are design choices, not mandatory Bootstrap breakpoints. Bootstrap's grid and display utilities can complement them, but our custom CSS owns the sidebar behavior.

### 6\. Create the JavaScript functionality

Open `wwwroot/js/site.js`.

This file will coordinate the sidebar and theme. Keeping the logic in one shared file means the behavior works consistently across MVC views.

The implementation uses three Local Storage keys or concepts:

* `themePreference` — stores `"light"` or `"dark"`.

* `sidebarState` — stores `"expanded"` or `"collapsed"`.

* `sidebarExpanded` — a temporary CSS class that allows tablet users to expand the sidebar without permanently changing the desktop preference.

The persisted preference remains separate from the temporary mobile drawer state. Opening the drawer on a phone should not change the user's desktop preference.

### Complete `wwwroot/js/site.js`

```javascript

"use strict";

document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", function () {
    // =====================================================
    // 1. FIND THE REQUIRED HTML ELEMENTS
    // =====================================================

    const root = document.documentElement;
    const body = document.body;

    const sidebar = document.getElementById("appSidebar");
    const mainContent = document.getElementById("mainContent");

    const collapseButton = document.getElementById("sidebarCollapse");
    const mobileToggleButton = document.getElementById("sidebarToggle");
    const mobileCloseButton = document.getElementById("sidebarClose");
    const overlay = document.getElementById("sidebarOverlay");

    const themeToggle = document.getElementById("themeToggle");

    // Use these keys consistently throughout the application.
    const THEME_KEY = "themePreference";
    const SIDEBAR_KEY = "sidebarState";

    const mobileBreakpoint = 768;
    const tabletBreakpoint = 992;

    if (!sidebar) {
        return;
    }

    // =====================================================
    // 2. SAFE LOCAL STORAGE HELPERS
    // =====================================================

    function readPreference(key) {
        try {
            return localStorage.getItem(key);
        } catch (error) {
            return null;
        }
    }

    function writePreference(key, value) {
        try {
            localStorage.setItem(key, value);
        } catch (error) {
            // The UI should still work if storage is blocked.
        }
    }

    // =====================================================
    // 3. THEME TOGGLE
    // =====================================================

    function applyTheme(theme) {
        const selectedTheme = theme === "dark" ? "dark" : "light";

        root.setAttribute("data-theme", selectedTheme);
        writePreference(THEME_KEY, selectedTheme);

        if (themeToggle) {
            const icon = themeToggle.querySelector("i");
            const isDark = selectedTheme === "dark";

            themeToggle.setAttribute(
                "aria-label",
                isDark ? "Switch to light theme" : "Switch to dark theme"
            );

            themeToggle.setAttribute(
                "title",
                isDark ? "Switch to light theme" : "Switch to dark theme"
            );

            if (icon) {
                icon.className = isDark
                    ? "bi bi-sun-fill"
                    : "bi bi-moon-stars-fill";
            }
        }
    }

    function initializeTheme() {
        const savedTheme = readPreference(THEME_KEY);

        applyTheme(savedTheme === "dark" ? "dark" : "light");
    }

    if (themeToggle) {
        themeToggle.addEventListener("click", function () {
            const currentTheme =
                root.getAttribute("data-theme") || "light";

            applyTheme(currentTheme === "dark" ? "light" : "dark");
        });
    }

    // =====================================================
    // 4. SAVE AND LOAD SIDEBAR STATE
    // =====================================================

    function saveSidebarState(state) {
        const selectedState =
            state === "collapsed" ? "collapsed" : "expanded";

        writePreference(SIDEBAR_KEY, selectedState);
    }

    function loadSidebarState() {
        const savedState = readPreference(SIDEBAR_KEY);

        return savedState === "collapsed" ? "collapsed" : "expanded";
    }

    // =====================================================
    // 5. APPLY DESKTOP SIDEBAR STATE
    // =====================================================

    function applyDesktopSidebarState(state) {
        const isCollapsed = state === "collapsed";

        root.classList.toggle("sidebar-collapsed", isCollapsed);
        root.classList.toggle("sidebar-expanded", !isCollapsed);

        if (collapseButton) {
            collapseButton.setAttribute(
                "aria-expanded",
                String(!isCollapsed)
            );

            collapseButton.setAttribute(
                "aria-label",
                isCollapsed ? "Expand sidebar" : "Collapse sidebar"
            );

            collapseButton.setAttribute(
                "title",
                isCollapsed ? "Expand sidebar" : "Collapse sidebar"
            );

            const label = collapseButton.querySelector(".sidebar-label");

            if (label) {
                label.textContent = isCollapsed
                    ? "Expand sidebar"
                    : "Collapse sidebar";
            }

            const icon = collapseButton.querySelector("i");

            if (icon) {
                icon.className = isCollapsed
                    ? "bi bi-layout-sidebar"
                    : "bi bi-layout-sidebar-inset";
            }
        }
    }

    // =====================================================
    // 6. MOBILE DRAWER OPEN/CLOSE
    // =====================================================

    function isMobile() {
        return window.innerWidth < mobileBreakpoint;
    }

    function openMobileSidebar() {
        if (!isMobile()) {
            return;
        }

        root.classList.add("mobile-sidebar-open");
        body.classList.add("mobile-menu-open");

        if (overlay) {
            overlay.hidden = false;
        }

        if (mobileToggleButton) {
            mobileToggleButton.setAttribute("aria-expanded", "true");
        }

        if (sidebar) {
            sidebar.setAttribute("aria-label", "Main navigation");
        }

        if (mobileCloseButton) {
            mobileCloseButton.focus();
        }
    }

    function closeMobileSidebar(returnFocus = true) {
        root.classList.remove("mobile-sidebar-open");
        body.classList.remove("mobile-menu-open");

        if (overlay) {
            overlay.hidden = true;
        }

        if (mobileToggleButton) {
            mobileToggleButton.setAttribute("aria-expanded", "false");

            if (returnFocus && isMobile()) {
                mobileToggleButton.focus();
            }
        }
    }

    if (mobileToggleButton) {
        mobileToggleButton.addEventListener("click", function () {
            const isOpen =
                root.classList.contains("mobile-sidebar-open");

            if (isOpen) {
                closeMobileSidebar(false);
            } else {
                openMobileSidebar();
            }
        });
    }

    if (mobileCloseButton) {
        mobileCloseButton.addEventListener("click", function () {
            closeMobileSidebar(true);
        });
    }

    if (overlay) {
        overlay.addEventListener("click", function () {
            closeMobileSidebar(true);
        });
    }

    // =====================================================
    // 7. COLLAPSE / EXPAND DESKTOP OR TABLET SIDEBAR
    // =====================================================

    if (collapseButton) {
        collapseButton.addEventListener("click", function () {
            if (isMobile()) {
                return;
            }

            const isCurrentlyCollapsed =
                root.classList.contains("sidebar-collapsed");

            const nextState = isCurrentlyCollapsed
                ? "expanded"
                : "collapsed";

            applyDesktopSidebarState(nextState);
            saveSidebarState(nextState);
        });
    }

    // =====================================================
    // 8. CLOSE MOBILE DRAWER AFTER NAVIGATION
    // =====================================================

    sidebar.querySelectorAll("a").forEach(function (link) {
        link.addEventListener("click", function () {
            if (isMobile()) {
                closeMobileSidebar(false);
            }
        });
    });

    // =====================================================
    // 9. ESCAPE KEY SUPPORT
    // =====================================================

    document.addEventListener("keydown", function (event) {
        if (
            event.key === "Escape" &&
            root.classList.contains("mobile-sidebar-open")
        ) {
            closeMobileSidebar(true);
        }
    });

    // =====================================================
    // 10. RESPONSIVE INITIALIZATION
    // =====================================================

    function handleViewportChange() {
        if (isMobile()) {
            // Mobile has its own drawer behavior.
            closeMobileSidebar(false);
            return;
        }

        // Clean up mobile-only state when leaving mobile width.
        closeMobileSidebar(false);

        if (window.innerWidth < tabletBreakpoint) {
            // Tablet starts compact unless explicitly expanded.
            // The desktop saved preference is left unchanged.
            applyDesktopSidebarState("collapsed");
        } else {
            // Desktop restores the saved user preference.
            applyDesktopSidebarState(loadSidebarState());
        }
    }

    // =====================================================
    // 11. STARTUP
    // =====================================================

    initializeTheme();

    // Desktop initial state uses the stored preference.
    applyDesktopSidebarState(loadSidebarState());

    // Correct the state for the actual viewport size.
    handleViewportChange();

    window.addEventListener("resize", handleViewportChange);

    // Keep a focus destination available for keyboard users.
    if (mainContent) {
        mainContent.setAttribute("tabindex", "-1");
    }
});
```

### How the JavaScript works

* Initialization: `DOMContentLoaded` waits until the HTML is available before the script searches for controls.

* Storage helpers: `readPreference()` and `writePreference()` protect against browser settings that prevent Local Storage access.

* Theme: `applyTheme()` sets the `data-theme` attribute and updates the button icon and accessible label.

* Sidebar persistence: `saveSidebarState()` stores the expanded or collapsed preference. `loadSidebarState()` restores it.

* Desktop and tablet: CSS classes control sidebar width and the main content margin.

* Mobile: `openMobileSidebar()` displays the drawer and overlay. `closeMobileSidebar()` reverses those changes.

* Keyboard support: Escape closes the mobile drawer.

* Responsive initialization: Resizing the browser switches between desktop, tablet, and mobile behavior.

The script does not need jQuery. It uses standard JavaScript DOM APIs.

Accessibility note: This drawer implementation supports basic keyboard controls and focus return, but it is not a complete modal dialog with a focus trap. For an application requiring strict modal-dialog behavior, use Bootstrap's responsive Offcanvas component or implement a tested focus-management pattern. Bootstrap's Offcanvas includes backdrop and accessibility conventions.

### 7\. How the Light/Dark theme integrates with the sidebar

The key design choice is that the theme is controlled by the HTML root:

`<html data-theme="light">` or `<html data-theme="dark">`.

The CSS variables then define the actual colors used by the sidebar, top bar, cards, text, and background.

### Light and dark palette

| UI element             | Light theme | Dark theme |
| ---------------------- | ----------- | ---------- |
| Sidebar background     | #FFFFFF     | #172033    |
| Sidebar text           | #475569     | #CBD5E1    |
| Main background        | #F5F7FB     | #111827    |
| Content card           | #FFFFFF     | #1F2937    |
| Active menu background | #EAF2FF     | #243C65    |
| Active menu text       | #1D4ED8     | #BFDBFE    |
| Borders                | #E2E8F0     | #374151    |

### Why CSS variables are useful

Instead of creating separate light-mode and dark-mode styles for every component, CSS variables allow the same component rules to adapt to the selected theme.

For example:

```css
.app-sidebar {
    background: var(--sidebar-bg);
    color: var(--sidebar-text);
}
```

When JavaScript changes `data-theme`, the browser recalculates the variables automatically.

### Preserve your existing theme system

If your current application already uses Local Storage for the theme, keep the same key name in `site.js`. In this tutorial, the key is `themePreference`.

If your existing code uses a different key, change `THEME_KEY` and the early theme initialization script in `_Layout.cshtml` to match it. Otherwise, the old toggle and the new toggle might appear to disagree.

The early inline script in the layout reads the saved theme before the stylesheet is painted, which helps reduce the flash of the wrong theme after page refresh.

For applications where users may disable JavaScript or Local Storage, keep a readable default theme and ensure core page content remains usable.

### 8\. Active menu detection: JavaScript vs. Razor vs. ViewContext

Three approaches are possible.

| Method      | Advantages                                          | Disadvantages                                |
| ----------- | --------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| JavaScript  | Useful for client-side navigation and dynamic menus | Requires URL parsing and can be more fragile |
| Razor       | Simple and explicit in the layout                   | Can become repetitive with many menu items   |
| ViewContext | Uses the current MVC route data on the server       | Still requires menu-specific logic           |

Recommendation: use Razor with `ViewContext.RouteData`. It is predictable for a conventional MVC application because the server already knows which controller and action are rendering the page.

The layout uses:

```cshtml
@{
    var currentController =
        ViewContext.RouteData.Values["controller"]?.ToString() ?? "Home";
}
```

And a menu link uses:

```cshtml
<a asp-controller="Employee"
   asp-action="Index"
   class="sidebar-link @(activeSection == "employee" ? "active" : "")"
   aria-current="@(activeSection == "employee" ? "page" : null)">
    <i class="bi bi-people" aria-hidden="true"></i>
    <span class="sidebar-label">Employees</span>
</a>
```

This highlights Employees for any action within `EmployeeController`, including `Details`, `Create`, and `Edit`.

If your application uses Areas, custom route values, or a single controller for several modules, adjust the active-menu logic accordingly. You can compare both controller and action for more precise matching.

### 9\. Save and restore sidebar state with Local Storage

Local Storage stores small key/value pairs in the browser and keeps them across page refreshes and browser sessions, until they are cleared. It is suitable for non-sensitive UI preferences such as themes and sidebar widths.

The core functions are:

```javascript
function saveSidebarState(state) {
    const selectedState =
        state === "collapsed" ? "collapsed" : "expanded";

    writePreference("sidebarState", selectedState);
}

function loadSidebarState() {
    const savedState = readPreference("sidebarState");

    return savedState === "collapsed" ? "collapsed" : "expanded";
}
```

The flow is:

User collapses sidebar

`saveSidebarState("collapsed")`

Local Storage saves `sidebarState`

On refresh, `loadSidebarState()` restores the preference

### Storage comparison

| Storage type    | Survives refresh? | Survives browser restart?             | Best use                               |
| --------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| Local Storage   | Yes               | Usually yes                           | Theme and sidebar preferences          |
| Session Storage | Yes               | No, generally tied to the tab session | Temporary per-tab UI state             |
| Database        | Yes               | Yes, across devices when user-linked  | Account-level preferences and settings |

Local Storage is scoped to the site's origin and is not automatically synchronized between browsers or devices. Do not store passwords, authentication tokens, or sensitive business data in it.

One important distinction in this implementation: on mobile, the drawer is temporarily opened or closed, but that state is not saved. The stored preference represents the desktop sidebar's expanded or collapsed state, not whether the mobile drawer is currently open.

